WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:06.480
If there’s anything that recent years have sadly&nbsp;
taught us, it’s that diseases are an insidious and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:12.960
formidable enemy that can take you by surprise.&nbsp;
Whether it’s bacterial, a virus, a fungal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:19.360
infection, or even an unfortunate genetic mutation&nbsp;
like cancer, Alzheimer’s, or Multiple Sclerosis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:25.040
sometimes dealing with a dangerous pathogen&nbsp;
could leave you praying for a run-in with SCP-682&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:30.400
after all, at least getting devoured by&nbsp;
a giant, evil reptile is a quick death.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:36.560
But take a page out of the SCP Foundation’s&nbsp;
book: Sometimes, the best response to an imminent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:43.120
threat is containment and research. One of the&nbsp;
first rules of combat is to know your enemy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:47.680
It may seem like a tall order to have&nbsp;
to write the book on dangerous diseases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:53.040
but thankfully, that book has&nbsp;
already been written: it’s SCP-1025&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:57.920
The Encyclopedia of Common Diseases. This&nbsp;
handy, reader-friendly volume will tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:02.480
you everything you need to know about all the&nbsp;
common maladies you may find yourself facing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:10.080
and a whole lot more. From the sniffles to lung&nbsp;
cancer, from a cold sore to necrotizing fasciitis.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.400
You may be thinking, “Why would I need some&nbsp;
dusty old book to tell me about diseases?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:19.920
I can always just go to WebMD.” But you don’t want&nbsp;
that. You know what the internet is like: They’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:24.480
just tell you you’re sick, and you don’t want&nbsp;
them putting silly ideas in your head like that.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:30.960
How about you open up to a random page and we’ll&nbsp;
take a look. There, see? The Common Cold. Classic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:36.080
Let’s see the description: “The Common Cold is a&nbsp;
virus that affects the upper respiratory tract.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:41.280
Its duration is typically a maximum of two weeks,&nbsp;
and its primary symptoms include headaches, muscle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:46.960
aches, a cough, a runny nose, raised temperature,&nbsp;
pressure in the ears or face, and a loss of taste&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:52.080
or smell.” See? Simple, straightforward,&nbsp;
and objective. Exactly what you need.&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:59.440
Did you just cough? That’s strange. Hmm, and come&nbsp;
to think of it, your nose is looking a little red.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:04.720
Do you need a tissue? Or perhaps a throat&nbsp;
lozenge? Your voice seems a little hoarse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:09.680
Perhaps you ought to take a break and lay down.&nbsp;
You really don’t look so good. Seems like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:14.880
common cold to us. Thankfully, you shouldn’t have&nbsp;
to worry about it for more than two weeks. Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:21.200
and that reminds me: There is a certain anomalous&nbsp;
effect to the Encyclopedia of Common Diseases.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:25.600
Whenever you read about a pathogen in&nbsp;
the Encyclopedia of Common Diseases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:32.160
you catch it, manifesting even its most severe&nbsp;
symptoms in an extremely brief period of time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:35.840
Of course, this isn’t that much of a problem&nbsp;
if you just read the article about having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:40.640
a sore throat or a stomach bug, but what&nbsp;
if you read the page for Ebola? Suddenly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:45.920
you’re half-dead and bleeding from the eyes. Or&nbsp;
the Bubonic Plague? Or a disease that humanity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:51.440
has never even faced before? And what&nbsp;
if, worst of all, the people who catch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:56.480
the diseases from this book continue to be&nbsp;
contagious to others? It could be the basis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:02.800
for a world-destroying biological weapon and at&nbsp;
the very least, a bonafide Keter-Class anomaly.

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:07.600
When the SCP Foundation got their hands on&nbsp;
this little hardcover anomalous nightmare,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:12.240
the researchers handling this thing realized&nbsp;
it needed to be kept under lock and key.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:15.840
A new provisional site was created&nbsp;
just to contain and research it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:20.480
without the risk of its anomalous effects&nbsp;
starting an unstoppable global pandemic.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:25.840
The base was an isolated underground bunker,&nbsp;
containing an even more high-security containment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:32.000
unit to store the book itself: A vault surrounded&nbsp;
by at least ten armed guards at any given time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.840
rotated twice weekly and checked&nbsp;
for any infectious agents.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:40.480
There was even a powerful thermite bomb
stored beneath the vault, set to go off and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:45.760
annihilate the entire chamber if the plausible&nbsp;
risk of a containment breach was detected.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:51.360
It may seem insane to put in this many security&nbsp;
fail-safes for a simple anomalous book, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:58.240
SCP Foundation has dealt with dangerous books&nbsp;
before. Take SCP-140, also known as an Incomplete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:03.440
Chronicle. This dangerous historical volume&nbsp;
chronicles the living history of the Daevites,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:08.880
an extinct culture of sadistic sorcerers&nbsp;
closely tied to the mythos of the Scarlet King.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:14.240
Whenever ink or blood is brought into the&nbsp;
presence of this book, it absorbs the material,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:19.760
expanding the Daevite’s history and bringing their&nbsp;
terrifying rule ever closer to the modern day.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:26.400
Or take SCP-3512, “The More You Know: A Pick-Up&nbsp;
Artist’s Bible”, a deadly instruction manual made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:31.680
by a mind-controlling group of interest known&nbsp;
as the Fifthists. This book gave its sinister&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:36.960
practitioners the tools to control the minds of&nbsp;
their potential romantic partners, often leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:44.240
to their deaths. While it may not be quite as&nbsp;
apocalyptic as SCP-1025 or SCP-140, the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:50.320
that there are so many in circulation still makes&nbsp;
it a dangerous threat. Point is, a book can be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:58.080
dangerous thing, especially if its pages have the&nbsp;
potential to unlock deadly diseases like SCP-1025.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:03.440
As you can expect with an infectious Keter-Class&nbsp;
anomaly of this magnitude, procedures around this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:09.120
one were rigorous. The site staff was a skeleton&nbsp;
crew, observing strict quarantine procedures to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:14.400
prevent any potential infection from leaking out&nbsp;
into the wider world. Research into the history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:19.600
of the book was inconclusive, despite a bevy of&nbsp;
Foundation resources being poured into finding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:26.240
any worthwhile leads on the author or publisher&nbsp;
of the book. Nothing. It was an absolute mystery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:32.480
who or what had put this XK-Class End of the World&nbsp;
Scenario waiting to happen here on earth. But now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:38.560
it was the job of these intrepid researchers to&nbsp;
discover the full extent of its potential dangers.

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:42.160
27 D-Class subjects were&nbsp;
allocated to the base for testing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:47.760
By the time the process was over, none of&nbsp;
these unlucky prisoners would remain alive.

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:52.160
The tests started much like this video: The&nbsp;
Researchers forced a D-Class to read the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:57.520
section on the Common Cold. Within two hours,&nbsp;
the subject began coughing. Upon being asked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:01.680
they also reported feeling physically achey,&nbsp;
though they attributed the ache to sleeping on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:06.720
an uncomfortable D-Class cot every night.&nbsp;
The researchers were concerned already:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:11.360
The incubation period for the common cold -&nbsp;
meaning the period a person is infected before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:17.360
showing active symptoms - can be as long&nbsp;
as three days. Two hours is unprecedented.

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:22.880
Next, a D-Class was told to read the section on&nbsp;
Chicken Pox, a common childhood malady that can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:28.480
considerably more dangerous in adulthood. Within&nbsp;
an hour of being exposed, the D-Class repeatedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:33.440
scratched no less than five points on her body&nbsp;
repeatedly. While she claimed this was because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:40.160
of the itchy D-Class jumpsuits, it was too eerily&nbsp;
close to a symptom of Chicken Pox to be ignored.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:45.440
The medical history of this D-Class also showed&nbsp;
that she’d suffered Chicken Pox in childhood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:51.200
suggesting that SCP-1025 infections have&nbsp;
the power to override previous immunities.

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:55.920
Next, things got a little more serious:&nbsp;
They tested the section on lung cancer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.920 --> 00:07:00.400
on a D-Class with no family history&nbsp;
of lung conditions. Not long after,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:06.160
the researchers noted that the D-Class coughed&nbsp;
an unusual number of times. Not only that, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:11.440
picked up irregularities in the D-Class’ breathing&nbsp;
that appeared to be characteristic of lung cancer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:16.160
While the D-Class did not report any feelings&nbsp;
of chest pain or discomfort, the researchers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:22.240
had him terminated and dissected. No tumors were&nbsp;
found. A researcher noted that they didn’t wait&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:28.400
long enough for proper tumor manifestation -&nbsp;
They knew they heard that D-Class coughing.

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:31.760
The researchers were so concerned&nbsp;
by the results of the previous test,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:37.200
they repeated it exactly with another D-Class,&nbsp;
who they then observed over a period of seven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:42.800
days to track tumor manifestation. Researchers&nbsp;
made a note of an unusual amount of coughing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:47.840
wheezing from the subject over this period&nbsp;
of time. The D-Class was then terminated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:55.200
and dissected - But surprise, surprise, no tumors.&nbsp;
Researchers began to consider the frightening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.200 --> 00:08:01.040
possibility that signs of SCP-1025-induced&nbsp;
illness disappear after the victims die,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:09.520
meaning that a pandemic started by SCP-1025 would&nbsp;
be impossible to properly track and respond to.

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:14.640
Things were only getting scarier and&nbsp;
scarier for the SCP-1025 researchers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:18.640
sequestered away in that&nbsp;
isolated Foundation bunker.

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:22.480
Researchers continued to delve further&nbsp;
into the lung cancer rabbit hole:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:26.800
This time, however, they didn’t wait for the&nbsp;
subject to die before looking at their insides.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:30.880
They instead performed a vivisection on&nbsp;
the subject, only to find that despite the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:36.080
victim still being alive and displaying some&nbsp;
of the key symptoms of lung cancer, there were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:41.760
no tumors. What could this mean? These diseases&nbsp;
weren’t behaving like any of their non-anomalous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:46.720
counterparts. What other differences could we&nbsp;
be dealing with here? Infectious lung cancer?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:51.360
And what if there are more books out there,&nbsp;
like time bombs, just waiting to go off?

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:58.160
The tests continued, getting more isolated and&nbsp;
more intense. In the end, the SCP-1025 site was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:04.560
staffed by only three researchers and two agents&nbsp;
acting as guards. D-Classes were sent in one by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:09.440
one to minimize ration requirements. It seemed&nbsp;
they could have been dealing with one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:15.200
dangerous anomalies of all time - even working&nbsp;
with it could be a trigger for the apocalypse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:19.520
if the proper containment and&nbsp;
quarantine procedures weren’t observed.

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:23.360
Things got so out of hand that a D-Class&nbsp;
without an appendix was told to read the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:29.040
section on Appendicitis, and researchers still&nbsp;
observed him exhibiting symptoms of the condition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:34.000
He was vivisected, and while the appendix was&nbsp;
still not present, the organs in the area where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:39.600
the appendix would have been seemed redder&nbsp;
than usual. Where would this nightmare end?

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:43.680
Well, it would actually end much sooner&nbsp;
than anyone involved would anticipate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:50.480
Despite never even reading SCP-1025 himself, a&nbsp;
researcher developed a strange persistent cough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:55.840
and was quarantined in a containment chamber by&nbsp;
his concerned colleagues. On the seventh day of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.840 --> 00:10:03.200
his confinement, he seemed to be slightly taller&nbsp;
than before - a symptom of SCP-016, a deadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:07.680
anomalous blood-borne pathogen that multiplies&nbsp;
wildly and rewrites the genetic structure of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:14.560
its host if placed into a high-stress situation.&nbsp;
This led to a number of terrifying revelations:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:19.280
Not only did the book have the potential&nbsp;
to inflict a number of anomalous diseases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:25.440
but it could also potentially do so by pure&nbsp;
proximity, even if you don’t actually read it.

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:31.200
What other anomalous diseases could be on the&nbsp;
table now? What about SCP-008, a terrifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:39.680
prion with both 100% virality and 100% lethality?&nbsp;
Or SCP-742, a retrovirus that slowly but surely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:45.840
transforms its victims into cannibalistic&nbsp;
monsters? Or even the horrifying SCP-217,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:51.840
the disease that slowly converts your&nbsp;
entire body into a mechanical nightmare?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:56.960
There would be no limit to the horrors&nbsp;
SCP-1025 could unleash on the world if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.960 --> 00:11:02.560
ever it got out. They needed to keep&nbsp;
this thing locked up at any cost.

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:07.600
That’s when the infected researcher&nbsp;
escaped, and all hell broke loose.

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:13.040
The isolated facility went dark for 72 hours.&nbsp;
After not being able to get into contact with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:18.720
any other Foundation bases during that time, a&nbsp;
recovery team was sent to investigate the mystery.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:23.840
One agent and one researcher were found in the&nbsp;
observation booth, wearing biological containment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:28.480
suits. Another agent was found crawling&nbsp;
through the air ducts with his handgun drawn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:33.920
a crazed look in his eye. An additional researcher&nbsp;
had locked himself in the barracks with a homemade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:38.640
flamethrower, paranoid about infection.&nbsp;
And the final agent was found dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:44.320
in a storage locker with some empty bottles and&nbsp;
ration packages, after having given the door an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:49.600
airtight seal to prevent infection&nbsp;
- then dying of suffocation inside.

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:56.640
The O5 Council shuttered the entire investigation.&nbsp;
SCP-1025 was downgraded from Keter to Safe Class,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.640 --> 00:12:01.360
then placed in a storage locker. The&nbsp;
whole thing was considered a wash.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:06.480
We know what you’re probably wondering: Why!?&nbsp;
If this anomaly was so incredibly dangerous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:11.440
why on earth would the O5 Council and the rest&nbsp;
of the Foundation be dealing with it so casually?

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:16.800
Well, that’s simple: The initial batch of&nbsp;
researchers had grievously misjudged the actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:24.160
anomalous effect of SCP-1025. It doesn’t give&nbsp;
diseases to those who read its text - instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.160 --> 00:12:30.000
it induces a form of “hypochondria by proxy”&nbsp;
to those around the reader. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:35.840
while SCP-1025 has no effect on the reader, it&nbsp;
makes everyone around them merely believe that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:41.040
they’ve come down with a terrible illness. The&nbsp;
few members of staff in that isolated site had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:47.280
been killing and dissecting D-Classes for no&nbsp;
reason. In effect, they’d all effectively gone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:54.960
mad due to the anomalous effects of SCP-1025 and&nbsp;
the paranoia induced by pressure and isolation.

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:59.760
Much like your average hypochondriac, they’d&nbsp;
warped the data to fit the worst possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:05.200
outcome rather than the more mundane and&nbsp;
likely. As an old medical expression goes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:08.240
“When you see hoofprints, think horses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:15.280
not zebras.” The entire thing had been a&nbsp;
terrifying waste of time, money, and human life.

00:13:15.280 --> 00:13:20.080
So perhaps, in the end, the Encyclopedia&nbsp;
of Common Diseases is a lot more like WebMD&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:25.200
than we thought. It’s not nearly as useful as&nbsp;
getting a consultation with an actual doctor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:31.360
and of course, it’s liable to put some&nbsp;
very strange ideas in your head...

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:36.880
Now go check out “SCP-2852 Sadistic&nbsp;
Madman Party Crasher | Cousin Johnny”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:43.520
and “SCP-701 - The Hanged King's Tragedy”&nbsp;
for more SCPs that’ll drive you to madness!

